# One line-ending convention for the whole repository.
#
# Without this file the split arrives out of the history and does not go away by itself: some files
# were stored with CRLF and some with LF. Nothing broke, but any editor with a habit of
# "normalising on save" turned a file over whole, and a commit ended up with thousands of changed
# lines with one real edit hiding among them. The second case is a clone with core.autocrlf=true
# (which is the default in the Git for Windows installer): then the LF files arrive as CRLF, and the
# very first save looks like a complete rewrite.
#
# text=auto  — everything is stored with LF in the repository: that way diffs and merges do not
#              depend on the platform, and that is the convention on GitHub;
# eol=crlf   — everything is checked out with CRLF in the working copy: the tool only works on
#              Windows, and that is its native convention.
* text=auto eol=crlf

# The icon is a binary file, and line-ending translation is bad for it. Git works this out by itself,
# but saying so outright is better than relying on guesswork.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
